  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nAt 1 PM CST, skies are mostly cloudy with temperatures slowly \nworking through the upper 40s into the low 50s. Winds are south at \n15-25 mph with gusts 30-40 mph.\n\nSimilar to a couple of days ago, winds are expected to drop off to \ngenerally 10mph or less over north central South Dakota after \nsundown tonight. There should also be lesser \ncoverage/thickness/duration of clouds around tonight, helping the \nradiative cooling process. These things promote fog formation, so \nthere is some fog mention in the grids for late tonight/early \nWednesday morning over portions of north central South Dakota. \n\nWhen the low pressure system currently over the intermountain west \nlifts out onto the plains tomorrow, it will track up into North \nDakota, taking pretty much all of the precipitation chances with it. \nBehind the system's cold frontal passage, southerly winds take on a \nwest-compenent and begin to increase. The highest confidence in wind \nspeeds getting to 30 mph or higher sustained with gusts to 45 mph or \nhigher shows up over north central South Dakota counties, so a Wind \nAdvisory has been issued for these counties (west river starting \ncloser to noon CST and east river starting closer to 6 PM CST). \nFactor in model guidance relative humidity down to between 25 to 35 \npercent over portions of central and south central South Dakota, and \nthe table will be set for elevated grassland fire weather \nconditions. The Day 2 Grassland Fire Danger outlook for Wednesday \ncontinues to maintain very high index across portions of central and \nnorth central South Dakota, with high index values everywhere else \nover the CWA. \n\nIt appears as though model guidance is trying to parse out two \ncirculations within the upper trof for the end of the week, with one \narea of low pressure lifting out and moving across the northern \nplains, while the other area of low pressure stays in the longwave \ntrof and surges out across the southern/central plains, heading for \nthe CONUS east coast over the weekend into Monday of next week. This \nchange from one big wound up system to two separate systems, along \nwith the northern plains-influencing low appearing a bit weaker and \ntracking further north/west across the region has resulted in less \nqpf within the WAA-forcing regime of the system. There is still a 20 \nto 40 percent chance of at least a tenth of an inch of rain falling \nacross portions of north central and northeast South Dakota and west \ncentral Minnesota Friday into Friday night. Guidance also has 50F-\nsomething dewpoints advecting up into the CWA on Friday, so thunder \nmention was added to the wx grids for Friday afternoon. Behind this \nlate week system, a less amplified upper level ridge re-establishes \nacross the middle of the CONUS with additional mid-level shortwaves \n(precipitation chances) occasionally running over/through the ridge, \nworking over the CWA during the days 5 through 7 forecast period.",
